  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1049 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nLatest GOES mid-level water vapor satellite imagery shows a\nrather sheared low over west Texas with dry air aloft over much of\nNew Mexico in association with a very positively tilted short-wave\nridge. Another pleasant fall afternoon is in store for the\nBorderland, with high temps running near to just a tad below\nnormal for mid-November. Dry air aloft, combined with surface\ndewpoints hanging in the low-mid 40s F overnight in some southern\nand western lowland locations, should allow for patchy fog\ndevelopment again tonight, though confidence is only moderate.\nThis is particularly so due to a northerly wind shift moving in\nlate tonight, which may keep the surface layer just stirred up\nenough to prevent much mist or light fog from forming. Not\nexpecting a repeat of any dense fog, at any rate.\n\nQuiet weather then persists from Veterans' Day through much of\nFriday, as the next ridge aloft crests over the area Thursday\nnight. As has been advertised, a southern stream mid/upper-level\nlow, progged to be near SAN early Friday, then opens up (high\nconfidence on this, per LREF H5 plots) and moves quickly along\nthe International Border. Mentionable PoPs spread west-to-east\nFriday night, with highest PoP and QPF in Hudspeth and Otero\ncounties. RRFS does indicate some CAPE Friday night, so a few \nrumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out. With the open-wave \nstructure of the trough, H7 temps only fall to 1-2C, so not \nexpecting much beyond perhaps a dusting of snow at the highest \npeaks (such as Sierra Blanca). Rain chances persist into Saturday\nafternoon in the higher terrain, aided a bit by a separate \ntrough/low traversing northern NM.\n\nAnother quiet period appears likely for much of Sunday through\nMonday, courtesy of the next ridge aloft. Confidence is \nincreasing in a more active pattern by the middle of next week, as\na long-wave trough set up shop along the West Coast, with \nshortwaves periodically ejecting ahead of it bringing \nprecipitation back into the picture.",
